#### 3.3.2 TOF PID

The time-of-flight TOF detector was fully installed in STAR in the year of 2010. Its role is to significantly improve particle identification. The TOF detector measures the time (\(t\)) taken by a track to traverse the distance (\(L\)) from the primary vertex to TOF. Knowing both \(t\) and \(L\), we can calculate the velocity (\(\beta\)) of each track by using the equation \(\beta\) = \(L/ct\), where \(c\) is the velocity of light.

Figure 3.4 shows the time-of-flight 1/\(\beta\) information as a function of track momentum (\(p\)). The particle species are well separated and the pion, kaon, and proton separation is very good to momenta above 1 GeV/c. The final cut that select kaons and pions is to require TOF \(|\)1/\(\beta\) -1/\(\beta_{exp}\)\(|\)\(<\) 0.03.

